<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caesar Rodney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caesar Rodney was an American politician from Delaware and a signer of the Declaration of Independence. On the night of 1–2 July 1776 he rode about 80 miles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> to break a tie in Delaware's delegation and cast its vote for independence. He later commanded Delaware's militia and served as the state's president from 1778 to 1781.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caesar-rodney.jpg" alt="Caesar Rodney" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maybe John Thomas Scharf · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CaesarRodney_(cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rodney was born on 7 October 1728 on Byfield, his family's plantation on St. Jones Neck in Kent County, Delaware. His grandfather William Rodney had come to the colonies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> in 1681–1682 and was speaker of the Colonial Assembly of the Delaware Counties in 1704; his mother was a daughter of the Rev. Thomas Crawford, Anglican rector of Christ Church at Dover. Byfield, a farm of 849 acres worked by enslaved labour, sold wheat and barley to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>.</p><p>He attended the Latin School of the academy and College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> until his father died in 1746, when the Orphan's Court placed him under the guardianship of Nicholas Ridgely. In 1755 he was elected sheriff of Kent County, then held posts as Register of Wills, Recorder of Deeds, justice of the peace and judge; from 1769 to 1777 he was an associate justice of the Supreme Court of the Lower Counties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Though he belonged to the Anglican gentry of Kent, Rodney sided with his brother Thomas with the minority Country Party, which favoured independence, working with Thomas McKean against George Read. He was a delegate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-congress">Stamp Act Congress</a> in 1765 and sat in the Delaware Assembly from 1761/1762 to 1775/1776, presiding on 15 June 1776, when it voted to sever ties with Parliament and the king. He signed the Declaration on 2 August, and a backlash in Delaware cost him election to the state's constitutional convention.</p><p>As president of Delaware from 31 March 1778 and major-general of its militia, he relied on his standing with the General Assembly, where real authority lay, and on the militia's loyalty. He gathered money, supplies and soldiers for the war and held down Loyalist activity, above all in Sussex County, scene of the Black Camp Rebellion of 1780. Failing health led him to resign on 6 November 1781.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rodney suffered from asthma all his life and for years from a facial cancer, wearing a green scarf to hide his face. He died on 26 June 1784, before the end of the Legislative Council session that had chosen him as its speaker. At his death he held 18 enslaved people; his will freed three of advanced age and set a schedule for freeing the rest. He lies in an unmarked grave on his family's former farm, and the stone at Christ Episcopal Church is a monument only. A statue of him on horseback stood in Rodney Square in Wilmington from 1923 until its removal in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caesar Rodney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 15 June 1776 the assembly declared the Lower Counties separate from both Britain and Pennsylvania, and <b>Caesar Rodney's</b> ride helped carry the vote for independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kent County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Kent County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.1,-75.5&amp;year=1764" aria-label="Kent County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2681 762 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Caesar Rodney"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3131 1032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3131 1032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.1,-75.5&amp;year=1764">What was here in 1764? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
